Absolutely — that’s a common source of frustration, especially when working from Windows clients like Git Bash. Here’s a clear, practical summary on how to prevent “incorrect line endings” for SSH keys:

Why it matters

OpenSSH expects ~/.ssh/authorized_keys to have Unix LF line endings.

Windows editors (Notepad, VS Code on Windows, Git Bash default) may save files with CRLF endings (\r\n).

If CRLF exists, the key is silently rejected — causing Permission denied (publickey) even if the keys match.

Prevention Strategies
1️⃣ Always generate keys on the system you’ll SSH from

Use Git Bash or Linux shell to generate keys:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "finance1@zerosix" -f ~/.ssh/finance1_ed25519


This ensures LF endings by default.

2️⃣ Use a Linux-friendly editor when modifying keys

Avoid Notepad or editors that default to CRLF.

Use:

nano, vim, vi (Linux/WSL)

VS Code → set EOL to LF: File → Preferences → Settings → End of Line → LF

3️⃣ Convert existing files to Unix format before copying

On the server, run:

sudo dos2unix /home/finance1/.ssh/authorized_keys


On Windows/Git Bash, you can also run:

dos2unix ~/.ssh/finance1_ed25519.pub

4️⃣ Copy keys using SSH-aware tools

Use ssh-copy-id instead of manually copy/paste:

ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/finance1_ed25519.pub finance1@192.168.56.71


This automatically handles LF line endings and permissions.

5️⃣ Set Git and editor defaults to LF

If you use Git:

git config --global core.autocrlf input


Converts CRLF → LF on commit but leaves LF untouched.

Prevents accidentally pushing CRLF keys.

✅ Best Practice:

Generate keys in Git Bash or Linux shell.

Never manually edit authorized_keys with a Windows editor unless you force LF.

Always verify with dos2unix before testing SSH.

